he following parametrically defined curve:\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to create a table of values and then sketch the curve. First, we work out the $x$-values using the formula $x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$ This gives\n\n| **$\\theta $** | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ | $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ | $\\pi $ | $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ | $\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $2$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $1$ |\n| $y$ | $$ | $$ | $$ | $$ | $$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nNow, we work out the $y$ values using $y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$ This gives\n\n| **$\\theta $** | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ | $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ | $\\pi $ | $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ | $\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $2$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $1$ |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $0$ | $-\\sqrt{3}$ | $-\\sqrt{3}$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nPlotting these points, we obtain the following curve:\n\n![](images/47/47615688bf550384de29a5e7e480bdab699b8eee994e5e28052e4d043e2db503.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106068 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105895 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fea43b6-fd7b-5edc-b6b7-7f8b2cbab502" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Type of Image Formed by a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bb34d14-4e6b-5921-bbae-0804ad06b4f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102295} {:answer/id #uuid "b2b33753-c1ca-54f2-b9c6-98ba27f6fa8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A rectangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102296} {:answer/id #uuid "cb1ac350-98a0-5f6c-8490-7f08a97c5de0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102297} {:answer/id #uuid "1338e2d6-476a-5cc4-a9f7-69b9fb7f0f6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102298} {:answer/id #uuid "39e1c3d2-cf9c-5c0f-ac61-180359ad5ac6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A point, different from the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102299}] :answer-field/id #uuid "98d6bd43-1322-57b8-8f87-0e031cdaba61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102294} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e72b8eba-d97d-557f-a8cb-b177b5a694e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401317} {:answer/id #uuid "239808c3-6662-5144-b216-bf28876d8c30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A rectangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401318} {:answer/id #uuid "d95c8151-cc29-5467-9bd0-4f7145939834" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401319} {:answer/id #uuid "a539e88c-cfb9-5231-b8f6-481fa85848fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401320} {:answer/id #uuid "ace8a67a-e74f-5f27-9582-9a2b3428a829" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A point, different from the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401321}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e72b8eba-d97d-557f-a8cb-b177b5a694e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401317} :answer-field/id #uuid "3dd41be4-e0dd-5cb0-830a-bc68816c31ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401316}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ba678a53-c013-5d44-b391-2d5cc0a9cc96" :question/math-academy-id "q-162150" :question/problem "![](images/e2/e2ab6906100a96a123b4b1520613f1e38fe2c7e77af390a8efe0596a2861727d.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nSince $T$ is the zero matrix, the image of our object under the action of $T$ is the origin."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281389 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105899 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f78fe749-e37e-53f2-879f-4c36931c1d6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Segment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34dc3d85-bf7d-5a32-bc17-8ac750c355ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279049} {:answer/id #uuid "642b22ea-1596-5521-b3ff-b9cef628875f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279502} {:answer/id #uuid "846472d7-37b6-51a7-af37-dc5316fa1feb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279779} {:answer/id #uuid "9500f03a-d333-5927-bed1-97f253541b9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279916} {:answer/id #uuid "dbb56e0e-2818-55a9-ad7f-03187608868a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280532}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dbb56e0e-2818-55a9-ad7f-03187608868a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280532} :answer-field/id #uuid "678b25a4-44dd-597e-88cf-490c50666c6d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281013}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d31dc5a7-37f0-52db-ac08-e744b2aa0398" :question/math-academy-id "q-162161" :question/problem "The equation below describes all the points on the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ where $P(3,0,-2)$ and $Q(1,1,-1).$\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ b \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [c,1]\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\nr=p+tv\\,⟹\\,r=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand the vector equation of the  line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=3+1+0=4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281390 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105899 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f78fe749-e37e-53f2-879f-4c36931c1d6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Segment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e7abf52-260b-5871-877d-d3cd9af93b7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278723} {:answer/id #uuid "4b3a8c37-ac70-5d4e-8764-2be1866f7061" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279265} {:answer/id #uuid "7d8754e9-d561-500f-ae97-638bb9ed4546" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279715} {:answer/id #uuid "8c01281f-bb41-5e46-8e01-ab1d074c18dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279838} {:answer/id #uuid "a1ae0f7a-ba36-5566-b7b7-82bb318a9f44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d8754e9-d561-500f-ae97-638bb9ed4546" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279715} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f5aec32-55d7-56a5-a482-291347058259" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280942}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c6ef52c7-4e9c-5db7-b87c-ad4749b7588e" :question/math-academy-id "q-162165" :question/problem "The equation below describes all the points on the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ where $P(0,-2,2)$ and $Q(1,-1,1).$\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ b \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [c,1]\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\nr=p+tv\\,⟹\\,r=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand the vector equation of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=2+1+0=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281391 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105899 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f78fe749-e37e-53f2-879f-4c36931c1d6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Segment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0acdb20a-bd99-5fd1-aefb-90dba9fa2833" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278696} {:answer/id #uuid "93028034-a984-5264-8d67-bb78f8f934a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279898} {:answer/id #uuid "ad375a48-0f06-5f5d-a1d5-0f4984254409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280125} {:answer/id #uuid "d9bc1ba0-2626-5083-a821-a634e14afb68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280515} {:answer/id #uuid "f8257693-9258-5a14-bd35-cec23355b615" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280770}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8257693-9258-5a14-bd35-cec23355b615" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280770} :answer-field/id #uuid "3582b34d-fad6-5c20-b7b8-eaa6ab83ca80" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280928}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6982b399-45f0-5a83-aeee-4f0df799362d" :question/math-academy-id "q-162167" :question/problem "The equation below describes all the points on the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ where $P(-3,5,0)$ and $Q(0,-3,2).$\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -8 \\\\ b\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [c,1]\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\nr=p+tv\\,⟹\\,r=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand the vector equation of the  line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=(-3)+2+0=-1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372000 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179773 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1577f765-f98c-5020-8e45-8da2c5f092e6" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Area Scale Factor of an Affine Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41c17750-e31e-532b-ae8d-7979de4d8d78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372002} {:answer/id #uuid "ad0c1dd5-b9ea-567c-a97b-f4096b3e1a5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372003} {:answer/id #uuid "5e0e426b-b2e9-58a1-91ce-578d1bd6cc16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372004} {:answer/id #uuid "1a41d7c7-bfb3-558c-bcd5-8d233c807fdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372005} {:answer/id #uuid "681b6932-d634-563f-b59e-a68a14cc53c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372006}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "681b6932-d634-563f-b59e-a68a14cc53c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372006} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2e7db5e-75b3-5228-9ad8-cef0891cba6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372001}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d122e060-ffdf-5149-8bb7-a8a052fb44e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-162172" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWhat is the area scale factor of the transformation?" :question/worked-solution "The matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}|=2\\cdot (-1)-3\\cdot 1=-5.\n$$\n\nSo, the area scale factor of the transformation is\n\n$$\n|det(A)|=|-5|=5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372007 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179773 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1577f765-f98c-5020-8e45-8da2c5f092e6" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Area Scale Factor of an Affine Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4dd51353-621e-56ae-bd7e-c1dcf4750e8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372009} {:answer/id #uuid "4692f893-08bf-51dc-8c1d-be1f128421c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372010} {:answer/id #uuid "dc922160-ef0e-51c1-bb3d-1ca4475e6dc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372011} {:answer/id #uuid "b3632d7d-0532-52f8-932d-ff5cdd002973" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372012} {:answer/id #uuid "2a9caf61-79c1-50ad-a6f3-d6e5f8d0d815" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372013}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4dd51353-621e-56ae-bd7e-c1dcf4750e8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372009} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab22a8ca-8854-500e-8a30-6e7d2bafc9e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372008}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7d4e1db4-c725-5d09-875d-b4ebdbd932b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-162175" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWhat is the area scale factor of the transformation?" :question/worked-solution "The matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|=1\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 1=3.\n$$\n\nSo, the area scale factor of the transformation is\n\n$$\n|det(A)|=|3|=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281406 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105895 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fea43b6-fd7b-5edc-b6b7-7f8b2cbab502" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Type of Image Formed by a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a6e4373-347b-586f-904c-be59818f4945" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279110} {:answer/id #uuid "5d56f302-e7a0-5f70-b0e0-272d1328de15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A pentagon" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279448} {:answer/id #uuid "dbf17246-a02f-5adb-adca-449dd47e93a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A point, different from the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280536} {:answer/id #uuid "e92272a3-0f88-52d3-9b2f-9e2c60c5273f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A rectangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280642} {:answer/id #uuid "f3652a9c-788f-5fa8-a9ee-b638c4ee6bba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280719}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a6e4373-347b-586f-904c-be59818f4945" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279110} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4f360b7-8b35-5cc3-a7cb-82ef205491ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281270}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0a4d15c5-f7e2-5db8-ae64-0fe650e3a234" :question/math-academy-id "q-162199" :question/problem "![](images/eb/eb1247ddcaaf12a342aa036550ed4f9f7daaeb6037e7ebbab0de619bb6975dbe.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the pentagon shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nSince $T$ is the zero matrix, the image of our object under the action of $T$ is the origin."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106069 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105895 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fea43b6-fd7b-5edc-b6b7-7f8b2cbab502" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Type of Image Formed by a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "148840cb-8cea-5745-9f92-680dd7f0ea95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278774} {:answer/id #uuid "5bb3d88b-4028-5ec9-80c5-a2ea9fc98832" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A point, different from the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279438} {:answer/id #uuid "aa7deda2-0f82-5af4-aed7-d429a9518b5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280104} {:answer/id #uuid "b4822b66-d6bb-592a-a36a-a168beb56876" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280182} {:answer/id #uuid "b8ad46d1-cb7b-5e5e-844d-6c83af425320" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A quadrilateral" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280215}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8ad46d1-cb7b-5e5e-844d-6c83af425320" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A quadrilateral" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280215} :answer-field/id #uuid "47cd30e7-fd7a-5e03-89d2-7a44f08b81a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280953}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0f03e3e5-8f9e-5206-8323-aab2c0298778" :question/math-academy-id "q-162206" :question/problem "![](images/e6/e6d0ce147aec90c3840a893e36596fbadf981ff96559f65d055f4486406cca7f.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the quadrilateral shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a nonsingular matrix. Therefore, the image of our object under the action of  $T$ is a quadrilateral."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389270 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171914 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c2c755c-59e0-5048-b20c-507f13fce441" :knowledge-point/title "Geometric Interpretation of the Roots of Unity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25fb5ca3-d3d8-556c-bfbd-18a27abab0d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c7/c70d25ac3a5ddb2d6765ef8a22f5e9774c9be06dd909d7079c3a361157881bb5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389272} {:answer/id #uuid "ddc77107-0356-5a3b-87bc-0fc7ca635b61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/91/9111f21143412d791b0195722da4f6d461444f386efd9baf609c70a4fe709fd8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389273} {:answer/id #uuid "34e1b08c-8354-55c3-a97a-4c32cfaf6d5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ba/bae536e1ec12168c413a6387f3106512173a34d92daae9ad6218040e7336c7ad.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389274} {:answer/id #uuid "fa39b0ed-b99c-5c6b-b5b4-628e084b1f3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/92/923d1a1b87833551ae6c451f15a6b5d8bdd75623e8d5c20e86357894bc24683d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389275} {:answer/id #uuid "213fcb6f-5b6e-56c4-9475-6d78a1c3d554" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e1/e11d63b3127cf3a162223ae7fca5e875e02622ec21480edd3fbc6cdddb039eae.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389276}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25fb5ca3-d3d8-556c-bfbd-18a27abab0d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c7/c70d25ac3a5ddb2d6765ef8a22f5e9774c9be06dd909d7079c3a361157881bb5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389272} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce62dc5a-db8c-574f-9cb2-3491413a4d60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389271}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d172d3ec-37f8-5fb9-8ea7-85c7f30ed514" :question/math-academy-id "q-162239" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams shows all the $3$rd roots of unity?" :question/worked-solution "Geometrically, the $3$rd roots of unity have the following properties:\n\n- all of them lie on the unit circle centered in the origin,\n\n- one of the roots is $1,$ corresponding to the point $(1,0)$ on the complex plane,\n\n- the roots form an equilateral triangle (regular $3$-sided polygon).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n![](images/c7/c70d25ac3a5ddb2d6765ef8a22f5e9774c9be06dd909d7079c3a361157881bb5.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389277 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171914 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c2c755c-59e0-5048-b20c-507f13fce441" :knowledge-point/title "Geometric Interpretation of the Roots of Unity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d450233-f778-5146-bb61-500a2a3edb67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/82/82682434de1fe7e9c7f086334f0ef7e0a7b2fde3d64efd7234ea10f1fb6c3b4f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389279} {:answer/id #uuid "ab2665ec-9150-5288-8394-5f9c6a6cd1c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/92/923d1a1b87833551ae6c451f15a6b5d8bdd75623e8d5c20e86357894bc24683d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389280} {:answer/id #uuid "0820dca7-deb5-5697-9159-8f0f4f3fcbc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/81/812c019fe97965e1757b9cf76c18439b69f3d8548b3be1b04530ee0b0e1ad4f4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389281} {:answer/id #uuid "5fbc07a1-e889-568b-82e7-6e63688d1848" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bf/bf2edb811442fc770f6f3c119c06bca3c17d30537f1941448ddb6ab0afa3eb8a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389282} {:answer/id #uuid "ffe48311-c82e-59ca-9751-9099ff7e1583" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0e/0e766db6832d6c8d2ad052f57ffc4f112ec995bd1462fb6a0998abf72c5d2059.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389283}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5fbc07a1-e889-568b-82e7-6e63688d1848" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bf/bf2edb811442fc770f6f3c119c06bca3c17d30537f1941448ddb6ab0afa3eb8a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389282} :answer-field/id #uuid "34b0aa03-9887-507d-915d-6585c4342069" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389278}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c0feb5e9-8cc6-5750-8eab-e0b55e158194" :question/math-academy-id "q-162241" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams shows all the $5$th roots of unity?" :question/worked-solution "Geometrically, the $5$th roots of unity have the following properties:\n\n- all of them lie on the unit circle centered in the origin,\n\n- one of the roots is $1,$ corresponding to the point $(1,0)$ on the complex plane,\n\n- the roots form a regular pentagon (regular $5$-sided polygon).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n![](images/bf/bf2edb811442fc770f6f3c119c06bca3c17d30537f1941448ddb6ab0afa3eb8a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281431 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100819 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a32f8ec3-ac69-5b6a-9937-1093556d24d7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a Trigonometric Equation Containing Sine or Cosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29053ade-ca82-5bab-aa52-fc1416a92dca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278956} {:answer/id #uuid "327f7f81-fa2d-554c-9d59-a0238b6b5c56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279034} {:answer/id #uuid "381d375a-d282-5558-af67-c3f309ae7ea8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279080} {:answer/id #uuid "64e63cbb-8403-5bbf-a00e-fae8cd3dad71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=n\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279509} {:answer/id #uuid "e0ad06ee-b6ca-5ac4-b103-4ef2e8096fc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280579}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0ad06ee-b6ca-5ac4-b103-4ef2e8096fc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280579} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7af4244-b19c-5140-981a-ed78c0638c37" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d9834690-2d79-5a22-9e52-3ff6dc17940f" :question/math-academy-id "q-16227" :question/problem "What is the general solution to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=1?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(1)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nThe sine function has a period of $2\\pi .$ Hence, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389284 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087788 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffddeaa7-a247-54b0-af4b-a29d9bbeee6a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maclaurin Expansion of a Sum of Functions Using Standard Maclaurin Expansions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "124f214f-d111-5270-8f5a-99d12cd7c085" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)=1+2x-2{x}^{2}-\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389286} {:answer/id #uuid "a3f8991a-ae24-560d-aaad-e0f0da3a2a44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)=1+2x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{8{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389287} {:answer/id #uuid "a3ffedca-2ff3-52db-bf69-7683ee3d3b9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)=1+4x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389288} {:answer/id #uuid "2557177c-b9c2-5021-9849-d0b2d282ff54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)=1+2x-2{x}^{2}-\\frac{8{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389289} {:answer/id #uuid "ab5f74b7-8408-5d07-a163-73944c92bf19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)=1+2x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389290}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab5f74b7-8408-5d07-a163-73944c92bf19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)=1+2x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389290} :answer-field/id #uuid "9eadffca-ae05-5f18-bf56-b5c8a1797f1e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389285}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a566bf55-85d0-5a70-9fbb-f45650b09aa0" :question/math-academy-id "q-16230" :question/problem "Find the first four non-zero terms of the Maclaurin expansion of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).$" :question/worked-solution "The Maclaurin expansion of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & =x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}+\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5!}-⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReplacing $x$ with $2x$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & =(2x)-\\frac{(2x)^{3}}{3!}+\\frac{(2x)^{5}}{5!}-⋯ \\\\  & =2x-\\frac{{2}^{3}{x}^{3}}{6}+\\frac{{2}^{5}{x}^{5}}{120}-⋯ \\\\  & =2x-\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{4{x}^{5}}{15}-⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe Maclaurin expansion of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4!}-⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the expansions for $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & =(2x-\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{4{x}^{5}}{15}-⋯)+(1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4!}-⋯) \\\\  & =1+2x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389291 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171914 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c2c755c-59e0-5048-b20c-507f13fce441" :knowledge-point/title "Geometric Interpretation of the Roots of Unity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36267af7-3b1c-53e8-8254-4652eb19b61b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/51/51ac4ad5bbc626c58ddbb6efd8c06bb665ef0849591dd846dd959756ea6e41b6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389293} {:answer/id #uuid "ae188fc3-ca39-5ffc-ab80-585fcf2025f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/73/73ac4fabf63cec9e0639bd2213bf2c4df4030ae0c143e0747ebde469418459d6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389294} {:answer/id #uuid "c2b96a31-29a9-5690-9d1f-f4a0e63422a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/71/717710d10e5f9919aa44a9e499509e19790e630027693f3dd72d8b2fd7caee2f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389295} {:answer/id #uuid "a562f990-eae4-516e-aae9-54b81af106e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/40/40af68e4d6e32b333a539fe33dc51ab52a77de3b8db12f930986e468cb033378.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389296} {:answer/id #uuid "b2fc1dee-e469-53cf-9212-2e5f92188e36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ea/ea44776f2632c23a4f52f50282d69492d2948722590ca5dbcaff619ae5079f7e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389297}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36267af7-3b1c-53e8-8254-4652eb19b61b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/51/51ac4ad5bbc626c58ddbb6efd8c06bb665ef0849591dd846dd959756ea6e41b6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389293} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb58a3f9-1543-53b6-8d35-b52ef0687fb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389292}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "07999dfb-3631-5698-9dcf-dad1b47402f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-162310" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams shows all the $8$th roots of unity?" :question/worked-solution "Geometrically, the $8$th roots of unity have the following properties:\n\n- all of them lie on the unit circle centered in the origin,\n\n- one of the roots is $1,$ corresponding to the point $(1,0)$ on the complex plane,\n\n- the roots form a regular octagon (regular $8$-sided polygon).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n![](images/51/51ac4ad5bbc626c58ddbb6efd8c06bb665ef0849591dd846dd959756ea6e41b6.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389298 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171914 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c2c755c-59e0-5048-b20c-507f13fce441" :knowledge-point/title "Geometric Interpretation of the Roots of Unity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8698d81c-07f1-58ee-9dd6-e41a3eaa29dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/59/59cc6771efc546ca0b6ff4fe7b9fd7a1e7794c20240052c3d8e4e29ad114ec8d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389300} {:answer/id #uuid "07eaee6d-65d6-5afc-9448-c3466e6e1563" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d8/d8a917138beb862f6816fc059847659b1ba11e17d6a7a4906309b021baf1d3e5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389301} {:answer/id #uuid "b0cc1df6-2564-53f0-b5cb-5a4e1d5c3a28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f1/f15f43e3663cfee8328135cb30894c4f268004f4bb74c838cb1bbf2c654c19f9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389302} {:answer/id #uuid "5834d494-4bff-53e7-8745-4b2b513b127b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b4/b4510002c6e424de6ea16f3037237ea9628b0ddd61f7fa4643d412b66b4fd749.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389303} {:answer/id #uuid "1bf18620-f54f-5e1e-8a0a-daf6f078a0ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/05/0525436f0f1ea9283c783bf590ae6dd58408ba5d5e2e6f69fc83af86e470864d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389304}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07eaee6d-65d6-5afc-9448-c3466e6e1563" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d8/d8a917138beb862f6816fc059847659b1ba11e17d6a7a4906309b021baf1d3e5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389301} :answer-field/id #uuid "e17095f8-ba86-50e5-a8b1-f267ffaff592" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389299}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f64f7f30-ad11-543c-8e4a-74267cbdc5a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-162311" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams shows all the $9$th roots of unity?" :question/worked-solution "Geometrically, the $9$th roots of unity have the following properties:\n\n- all of them lie on the unit circle centered in the origin,\n\n- one of the roots is $1,$ corresponding to the point $(1,0)$ on the complex plane,\n\n- the roots form a regular nonagon (regular $9$-sided polygon).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n![](images/d8/d8a917138beb862f6816fc059847659b1ba11e17d6a7a4906309b021baf1d3e5.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281446 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192803 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dab23d8f-dc62-5862-b5e9-e7d5602ad42c" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Root of Unity Using Its Principal Root"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a4e8559-e1f9-5eaf-8f41-2cc950f70eac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279555} {:answer/id #uuid "851cdbdc-0097-5665-9a3b-e276bd7b1aeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279788} {:answer/id #uuid "87e5e19c-1dc1-5fe5-a071-f9fd7d84664a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279805} {:answer/id #uuid "b6c438fb-9a53-510b-b9cc-b42b8c92a314" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280195} {:answer/id #uuid "cd52a6f5-7d83-50ec-a4b5-3722b4271a9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87e5e19c-1dc1-5fe5-a071-f9fd7d84664a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279805} :answer-field/id #uuid "17d2ce1b-51be-5bf6-9ddb-b26294e30ce2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280874}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5358c811-fb51-54cc-b39e-4cede46315bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-162343" :question/problem "The $6$th root of unity ${e}^{4\\pi \\text{i}/3}$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{e}^{4\\pi \\text{i}/3}={z}^{p},\n$$\n\nwhere $z$ is the principal $6$th root of unity. What is the value of $p?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $6$th roots of unity can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}/6}\n$$\n\nwhere $k=0,1,…,5.$ \n\nWe obtain the *principal* $6$th root of unity by substituting $k=1:$\n\n$$\nz={e}^{2\\cdot 1\\cdot \\pi \\text{i}/6}={e}^{\\pi \\text{i}/3}\n$$\n\nWe can write the given root of unity as a power of the principal root as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4\\pi \\text{i}/3} & =({e}^{\\pi \\text{i}/3})^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281475 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100822 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff04ceeb-c9f6-5562-b494-204eb62d7f5b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of the Points of Intersection of a Trigonometric Curve and a Horizontal Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08f2c6ea-935c-52be-abaa-4c332cc0fd57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278677} {:answer/id #uuid "17605140-bedc-5fff-9676-0d338f2dc7c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278801} {:answer/id #uuid "1c12e4f2-ff20-5fbd-9fc0-8ab5b451aaad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278849} {:answer/id #uuid "31de253a-4fc8-52c3-9e70-43605efad23c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279029} {:answer/id #uuid "9311b1e5-983f-56a8-b6d1-330651531b3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17605140-bedc-5fff-9676-0d338f2dc7c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278801} :answer-field/id #uuid "175088b2-d5c3-52c9-bc4e-8315e0bcb28d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280873}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "62746e40-0193-5ca9-870e-c828dbc32d5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-16245" :question/problem "![](images/35/350458d29c8d214f44b175e28a6f43accd7ce064187cf755324cbe99d53eb7ad.png)\n\nThe graph shows a portion of the curve $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ and $y=-1.$ Which of the following generates the $x$-values of all of their points of intersection? Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "At the intersection points, the $y$-values are the same for both graphs. This gives the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-1.$ \n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1)=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nThe tangent function has a period of $\\pi .$  Hence, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401364 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115356 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "553f7b9d-c387-59b0-a0d6-9624d012e414" :knowledge-point/title "Domains of Functions With Trigonometric Components"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8b48905-c922-5f0b-904d-031f2f366d2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401366} {:answer/id #uuid "07837613-c6cb-599f-a1fe-60f06f0661aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi +2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401367} {:answer/id #uuid "823a0eb9-049b-54c7-87a1-4762c639c714" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401368} {:answer/id #uuid "e5f740a3-7481-581f-876f-a404eea190e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401369} {:answer/id #uuid "730966c2-9ffc-5f1d-9533-075afe811e99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401370}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "730966c2-9ffc-5f1d-9533-075afe811e99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401370} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2067c3d-0d1e-5094-9c41-cc29fa1d1f8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401365}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c16eaa20-dd31-5858-a148-07284cbf2219" :question/math-academy-id "q-162632" :question/problem "The domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)=2{t}^{3}\\,i+(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\,k$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne .\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression? You may assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component $2{t}^{3}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$, $t\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer.\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401378 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115356 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "553f7b9d-c387-59b0-a0d6-9624d012e414" :knowledge-point/title "Domains of Functions With Trigonometric Components"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ede8e16-494c-5ead-ba64-5838ef07def5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401380} {:answer/id #uuid "4bb8a9be-6f0a-565b-b6b2-11bc4789ac9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401381} {:answer/id #uuid "70995bca-c45d-5efe-9c86-d0ad7a6058b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401382} {:answer/id #uuid "50f624a9-d806-5717-a7e5-2f6fcc28ebd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401383} {:answer/id #uuid "2e3fe947-d5c0-5bd5-8ca5-786edda0e0d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401384}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e3fe947-d5c0-5bd5-8ca5-786edda0e0d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401384} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a56ee90-9fa8-5ebf-a7cd-271d63b533b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401379}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7adf6f23-c582-5c46-b1a7-7edbfe6210af" :question/math-academy-id "q-162637" :question/problem "The domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)=(1-{t}^{3})\\,i+3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,j+\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,k$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne .\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression? You may assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component $1-{t}^{3}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$, $t\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer.\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component $\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281519 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115355 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a1499c-080d-5008-bcc3-492ef469d3dc" :knowledge-point/title "Domains of Functions With Exponential and Logarithmic Components"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56e5c99c-8b6a-5013-8eac-a3f5875b7e02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\in (3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279387} {:answer/id #uuid "570a0757-1af5-5ac0-bafe-77a250c5a1c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\in (-3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279391} {:answer/id #uuid "711de546-82a1-5b66-986c-be2f0a791fcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\in (-\\infty ,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279618} {:answer/id #uuid "934f4336-4dd2-5616-a126-27ad5a79485a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\in (0,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279901} {:answer/id #uuid "d2f20920-1178-5a0e-8537-c7d213dcabe0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\in (-\\infty ,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280452}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2f20920-1178-5a0e-8537-c7d213dcabe0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\in (-\\infty ,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280452} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd093b0b-20b6-5238-8364-92e525577812" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281191}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9cfd8b65-c745-5224-8ac7-8d2870d4816c" :question/math-academy-id "q-162656" :question/problem "Find the domain of the vector-valued function $f(s)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-s),\\,2-{s}^{3},\\,2s+1⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-s)$ is $s\\in (-\\infty ,3).$\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $2-{s}^{3}$ is $s\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component $2s+1$ is $s\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nThe intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/2f/2fa5c39e68278bdff8ff258f7e2d162956affe67a29119242f53416affee0277.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(s)$ is $s\\in (-\\infty ,3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281528 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115354 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "461c0f55-cb85-511e-b8cb-be11f8a3c250" :knowledge-point/title "Domains of Functions With Reciprocal and Radical Components"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1145d800-e17b-5b19-a219-a290ca52a33e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (-1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278750} {:answer/id #uuid "3fea574f-e038-5d76-8957-afdd3ea45d30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in (-\\infty ,1]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279154} {:answer/id #uuid "883b6a5e-56e3-5b4a-baf4-b4515c492f1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in (-1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279809} {:answer/id #uuid "a0ee270a-85a1-5186-a8b5-3a0ed1a87222" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in [-1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280023} {:answer/id #uuid "c9bff1c4-353d-5e75-aa8e-6f9e13e49ad1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in (-\\infty ,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280357}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0ee270a-85a1-5186-a8b5-3a0ed1a87222" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in [-1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280023} :answer-field/id #uuid "3981d713-59f2-5fd3-a5f1-58bc416cbeea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280935}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5b637ea2-eaa0-55d8-b435-a20c614987db" :question/math-academy-id "q-162673" :question/problem "Find the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{t+1} \\\\ {e}^{t} \\\\ 2t\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "To find the domain of $f,$ we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components. \n\n- The domain of $\\sqrt{t+1}$ is $t\\in [-1,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of ${e}^{t}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of $2t$ is  $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nThe intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/3f/3f1ca1c3d6ffd56fe0686ee209c967775b5daf09a99e80a3f38ad0a56f4424ce.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is $t\\in [-1,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281532 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092349 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b16f563c-f4be-5883-8734-cbce48de4757" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Matrix That Represents a Combination of Three Linear Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05934a10-0ed3-5542-bb46-0f9d1a97bb6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278644} {:answer/id #uuid "0b4c7326-c31c-54c2-b8c5-b05c1d39ec4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278700} {:answer/id #uuid "5646678b-b0f1-5e11-9a16-3f01f9b65459" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279383} {:answer/id #uuid "76e539d7-7633-5374-86f9-6d954fa8f958" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279653} {:answer/id #uuid "d53f3dea-c416-544c-b31b-5ee524377b89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280474}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5646678b-b0f1-5e11-9a16-3f01f9b65459" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279383} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e3e03e8-92a5-53c7-a1d7-ee9ae71efd68" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280917}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9939f92b-9874-5d18-8b59-989570a881e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-162704" :question/problem "The matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nrepresents the linear transformation that acts on a vector in ${R}^{2}$ as follows (in the order given):\n\n- It rotates the vector  $\\pi $ radians counterclockwise about the origin, then\n\n- stretches the result by a scale factor of $3$ parallel to the $x$-axis, and finally\n\n- reflects the result about the $x$-axis.\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $P$ be the matrix that represents the rotation, $Q$ be the matrix that represents the stretching, and $R$ be the matrix that represents the reflection. These are given by the following elementary transformation matrices:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nHence, the combined transformation can be represented by the matrix $RQP:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RQP & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n$a+b=(-3)+1=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281558 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162154 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "900dcaf8-da39-5654-96cd-6aec89e379c9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Joint Probabilities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57ab92be-2107-5550-a809-529059249e91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279396} {:answer/id #uuid "93733b1c-0d9a-53b0-9a3f-5da14d0f1b83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279903} {:answer/id #uuid "a1598f80-0230-5989-a1fa-9d35bee1048e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280026} {:answer/id #uuid "b026cdca-5397-59bf-a2e8-c09bbb8a8352" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280153} {:answer/id #uuid "b837c811-5ec2-5691-b5bf-dd9fe9e21452" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.03" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280213}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93733b1c-0d9a-53b0-9a3f-5da14d0f1b83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279903} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b5a5bce-31ee-5ab8-974c-15db5cd460e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280881}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d8e27fa4-3108-5d1b-bf36-7c34fcdd2cd8" :question/math-academy-id "q-162901" :question/problem "The partially complete joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the independent discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is below. \n\n| $f$ | $Y=-1$ | $Y=1$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.03$ | $0.07$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=4$ | $∗$ | $∗$ | $0.9$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $?$ | $∗$ | $1$ |\n\nWhat is the value of $P(Y=-1)?$" :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nTherefore, since our random variables are independent, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,-1) & ={f}_{X}(2)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(-1) \\\\ P(X=2,Y=-1) & =P(X=2)\\cdot P(Y=-1) \\\\ 0.03 & =0.1\\cdot P(Y=-1) \\\\ P(Y=-1) & =\\frac{0.03}{0.1} \\\\  & =0.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365253 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179715 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c812adc-7418-5a76-9c7f-96c726c7ca0e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maximum Speed of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec26c32f-330e-5878-8370-d35956d6bd96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365255} {:answer/id #uuid "255624ce-60b8-5c54-9dfe-53912d1536ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365256} {:answer/id #uuid "fe85ed8c-5a74-5ea1-9034-77b240f6eb4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365257} {:answer/id #uuid "3a46d353-568f-565f-a23b-bcdc3c812f55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{13}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365258} {:answer/id #uuid "d7fcf9de-02cf-52a0-b525-2fc6833847e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{6}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a46d353-568f-565f-a23b-bcdc3c812f55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{13}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365258} :answer-field/id #uuid "95c44088-63d3-5d47-8f73-8487757bc6ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365254}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c0d1d1a5-d9cb-5570-acc7-da72657b9a2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-163548" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\ge 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ and the acceleration is $(4-x)\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has velocity $v=6\\,m/s$ at the origin, find the maximum velocity of the particle." :question/worked-solution "![](images/d4/d4e5b78932f8ff6155ac81e12538eb31ac211c9d27d6a2b0c67542527ccd3b2a.png)\n\nThe extrema of the velocity occur at the critical points of $v.$ Since the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(4-x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=4-x.\n$$\n\nTo find the moments where $v$ is a maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-x & =0 \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $x=4\\,m.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the first derivative test. We summarize the necessary information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,4)$ | $4$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $2$ |  | $6$ |\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ | $2>0$ | $0$ | $-2<0$ |\n| $v$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nWe conclude that the velocity is maximized when $x=4\\,m.$\n\nNext, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our initial equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=4-x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (4-x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=4x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=6\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =4x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(6)^{2} & =4(0)-\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2}+C \\\\ C & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =4x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+18 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =8x-{x}^{2}+36 \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+8x+36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can disregard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+8x+36}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{max}=v(4)=\\sqrt{-(4)^{2}+8(4)+36}=2\\sqrt{13}\\,m/s\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284390 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157424 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "037ff08b-3246-5eed-870b-b4951f319c96" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Hessian Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a7ccfc6-c980-5f87-98b3-cb27c26de86d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281784} {:answer/id #uuid "1cc546d0-b0b6-5368-af23-5ec0c2c96352" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281949} {:answer/id #uuid "48389a8d-c104-542e-8686-3b973c39c4df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282301} {:answer/id #uuid "7f03e21b-fba2-5b1e-bbaa-ba00d1112786" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282783} {:answer/id #uuid "c372c923-e6e7-5ef4-8346-000c6e272e18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283391}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f03e21b-fba2-5b1e-bbaa-ba00d1112786" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282783} :answer-field/id #uuid "533cc9b6-1b67-5a24-ae39-2f81ecaf9c2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284069}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "64116d30-10df-5392-b872-327ec4131913" :question/math-academy-id "q-163642" :question/problem "Given that \n${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}4xy-3{y}^{2} & 2{x}^{2}-6xy\\end{aligned}],$ \nevaluate the Hessian determinant of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(x)=det(\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(x))\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since\n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}4xy-3{y}^{2} & 2{x}^{2}-6xy\\end{aligned}],$\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=4xy-3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2{x}^{2}-6xy.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4xy-3{y}^{2})=4y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}-6xy)=4x-6y \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4xy-3{y}^{2})=4x-6y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}-6xy)=-6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4y & 4x-6y \\\\ 4x-6y & -6x\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(1,1)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(1,1)=4\\cdot (-6)-(-2)\\cdot (-2)=-28.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083564 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083299 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "223e29c9-1bb5-51ee-9ec8-4d5c15363951" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center of a Horizontal Hyperbola Given an Equation in Expanded Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "038e7ca3-9f2e-50ec-983a-de6bd000e63c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(7,-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281719} {:answer/id #uuid "26bdbef2-c47a-5d6c-aca0-ba40f2fbbbb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-6,-7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282026} {:answer/id #uuid "d72cdc8c-90d4-561e-9a5a-18d0d4a96d6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283563} {:answer/id #uuid "fe197ae0-a4c9-55f4-812e-62cf4e98d829" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-6,7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283919} {:answer/id #uuid "ffb7a6ad-44d9-55a0-9220-b166c5327e84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283932}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe197ae0-a4c9-55f4-812e-62cf4e98d829" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-6,7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283919} :answer-field/id #uuid "4859b225-780b-5365-b784-c694b9fa73c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284052}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "025d6071-d0ec-5817-8d91-2087900261c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-16381" :question/problem "What is the center of the hyperbola \n$6{x}^{2}-5{y}^{2}+72x+70y=59?$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTo rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in standard form, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}-5{y}^{2}+72x+70y & =59 \\\\ 6{x}^{2}+72x-5{y}^{2}+70y & =59 \\\\ 6[{x}^{2}+12x]-5[{y}^{2}-14y] & =59 \\\\ 6((x+6)^{2}-36)-5[(y-7)^{2}-49] & =59 \\\\ 6(x+6)^{2}-216-5(y-7)^{2}+245 & =59 \\\\ 6(x+6)^{2}-5(y-7)^{2} & =59+216-245 \\\\ 6(x+6)^{2}-5(y-7)^{2} & =30 \\\\ \\frac{(x+6)^{2}}{5}-\\frac{(y-7)^{2}}{6} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the center is $(-6,7).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088032 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087866 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7de671ee-7bba-54ce-bec2-d2d6f5569f4a" :knowledge-point/title "Writing First-Order Linear ODEs in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab721a7c-ebb0-5452-b247-5d3816198bf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + (4\\sin x)y = 1 + 3\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084927} {:answer/id #uuid "b319152d-c291-5494-a461-4791daa6c062" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + (4\\cos x)y = 1 + 3\\sin x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084928} {:answer/id #uuid "c17d0099-f48a-51bc-b6bf-feeec39e8bdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + (2\\sin x)y = \\frac{1 + 3\\cos x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084929} {:answer/id #uuid "2a507547-4026-5e0f-ae6c-972cd79efc1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + \\frac{1 + 3\\cos x}{2} = (2\\sin x)y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084930} {:answer/id #uuid "606289fa-36e0-57ab-b7c2-57b9dbbb4540" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + (2\\sin x)y = 1 + \\frac{3}{2}\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084931}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2e534d32-a3b5-5b67-a30a-d8fb0702e472" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084926} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4bcf009-6fca-5110-8ae8-643678b3cb9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)y=\\frac{1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401434 :task-item/_responses [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409291}]} {:answer/id #uuid "eb82a4b2-58af-556c-a366-f92624768704" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)y=1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401435} {:answer/id #uuid "f4a26a57-987c-5668-b531-e2f3db2b75bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)y=1+\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401436} {:answer/id #uuid "b6972414-a235-5987-855e-567ba571b605" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)y=1+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401437} {:answer/id #uuid "e08cc03c-cea4-583d-b68b-e5c8622f76e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}=(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401438}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4bcf009-6fca-5110-8ae8-643678b3cb9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)y=\\frac{1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401434} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef647b19-79e2-5b54-b8df-1bf5fe9db9b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401433}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e5c269fd-ead8-50e4-b16e-9d9e9b4a244c" :question/math-academy-id "q-16400" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1-4y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =1-4y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ 2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y & ={{\\frac{1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284444 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087731 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0adde8fd-26c3-52f5-9864-5e96641a8b63" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Complex Conjugate of a Complex Number With Negative Imaginary Part"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "263bdc6d-4fda-5948-a654-f5102a6ece85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{―}{z}=5+12\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282022} {:answer/id #uuid "b299723b-ff19-5b3d-b12a-1547439addaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{―}{z}=\\frac{1}{5-12\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283239} {:answer/id #uuid "dd9cb080-c86f-5905-9b32-c8840ca494dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{―}{z}=-5+12\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283625} {:answer/id #uuid "e96c448b-3d35-59a6-9940-d54682027729" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{―}{z}=5-12\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283726} {:answer/id #uuid "ec2ac6e6-d883-532d-b564-f3a895defbf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{―}{z}=-5-12\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283751}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "263bdc6d-4fda-5948-a654-f5102a6ece85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{―}{z}=5+12\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282022} :answer-field/id #uuid "812cc3a4-e03d-542a-b988-6dbc14db679b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284152}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f33a661d-acf6-551f-b7e5-6ad7d3cf9b2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-16414" :question/problem "What is the complex conjugate of $z=5-12\\text{i}?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the complex conjugate, we flip the sign of the imaginary part.\n\nIn the given number $z=5-12\\text{i},$ the imaginary part is $-12\\text{i}.$ Flipping the sign, we get $12\\text{i}.$\n\nTherefore, the complex conjugate is given by $\\overset{―}{z}=5+12\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372307 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092227 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d59733f-3396-5d59-98a9-3f2e0ccfbbc4" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area of the Image of an Object Under a Linear Transformation Given a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1217f57f-e5a4-57f9-b3f6-236425752482" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372309} {:answer/id #uuid "562a512e-8180-5112-8fed-52f3ad842838" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372310} {:answer/id #uuid "e90017fd-784b-5993-bd83-af65d1f64405" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372311} {:answer/id #uuid "be608145-09ba-5d55-ac0f-91e6733d6b81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "56\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372312} {:answer/id #uuid "c3f8ffee-fc72-5cd0-9217-9b10089f9cb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372313}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3f8ffee-fc72-5cd0-9217-9b10089f9cb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372313} :answer-field/id #uuid "2823d0f1-eb74-5f93-a7dc-d4991ade9c87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372308}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "10061e50-a893-52e0-a034-345aab66034e" :question/math-academy-id "q-164170" :question/problem "![](images/db/db042c1d6bc23ed95988fe1a83a565065928dca06ffe454225da6c638a70f741.png)\n\nConsider the shaded region $D$ shown above. What is the area of the image of $D$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ with the standard matrix $T,$ given below?\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T,$ we have the following connection between the area of $D$ and the area of the image $T(D)$ under the action of $T:$\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(D))=|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2}=9\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we get \n\n$$\ndet(T)=4\\cdot (-2)-(-3)\\cdot 2=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(D)) & =|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =|-2|\\cdot 9\\pi  \\\\  & =18\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284453 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087846 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "37a4168b-1425-5f3a-a577-4b5884906a52" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Sum of a Complex Number and Its Conjugate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06e16935-1003-52ed-a03b-f4c61dd870d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281751} {:answer/id #uuid "264a88bc-6062-5e3b-9a17-7a92ed55ba6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282024} {:answer/id #uuid "2cf06f2c-9f8f-538b-8f01-0758002d1584" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10+10\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282087} {:answer/id #uuid "5f45f379-dd47-579f-966c-3906aef12d7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282491} {:answer/id #uuid "719a6e30-a3a3-51da-9f47-cbd0d927b100" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282659}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f45f379-dd47-579f-966c-3906aef12d7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282491} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3b1957c-b96b-5d85-8ee8-bf171f645eb5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284281}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ffd9d977-ab54-5f18-98fb-c284e3e5b28b" :question/math-academy-id "q-16419" :question/problem "If $z=5+5\\text{i}$, calculate $z+\\overset{―}{z}.$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWhen we add a complex number and its conjugate, the result is always twice the real part of the complex number.\n\nFor the given complex number $z=5+5\\text{i},$ the real part is $Re(z)=5,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+\\overset{―}{z} & =2\\text{Re}(z) \\\\  & =2(5) \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nAlternatively, we can actually carry out the process of adding the complex number and its conjugate.\n\nTo obtain the conjugate of the complex number $z=5+5\\text{i},$ we flip the sign of the imaginary part and get $\\overset{―}{z}=5-5\\text{i}.$\n\nAdding, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+\\overset{―}{z} & =(5+5\\text{i})+(5-5\\text{i}) \\\\  & =(5+5)+\\text{i}(5-5) \\\\  & =10+\\text{i}0 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284455 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087847 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64cd3ef1-6d41-50c6-b09d-cb709d89281f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Product of a Complex Number and Its Conjugate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a6e2723-1d96-59f0-9ce8-1086dbae82be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "97\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282324} {:answer/id #uuid "988ae48c-0346-5d01-9514-34a4b4841eb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "85\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283008} {:answer/id #uuid "a395c26d-5b19-522a-a755-4f809cfa866c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283114} {:answer/id #uuid "ccc5556d-0f68-51f8-ad0f-405b5cda6250" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "97" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283476} {:answer/id #uuid "d33c6f49-90ce-5f06-aede-23fd08c90bdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "85" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283533}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ccc5556d-0f68-51f8-ad0f-405b5cda6250" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "97" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283476} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6d807f4-cf50-5ea5-a6ac-a321fddfc003" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284286}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c4891bd7-301f-542e-bf98-c65b22eb754e" :question/math-academy-id "q-16423" :question/problem "Given that $z=4+9\\text{i}$, calculate $z\\overset{―}{z}.$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWhen we multiply a complex number and its conjugate, the result is always the sum of squares of the real part and the imaginary part.\n\nFor the given complex number $z=4+9\\text{i},$ the real part is $\\text{Re}(z)=4,$ and the imaginary part is $\\text{Im}(z)=9,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\overset{―}{z} & =(\\text{Re}(z))^{2}+(\\text{Im}(z))^{2} \\\\  & ={4}^{2}+{9}^{2} \\\\  & =16+81 \\\\  & =97.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nAlternatively, we can actually carry out the process of multiplying the complex number and its conjugate.\n\nTo obtain the conjugate of the complex number $z=4+9\\text{i},$ we flip the sign of the imaginary part and get $\\overset{―}{z}=4-9\\text{i}.$\n\nMultiplying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\overset{―}{z} & =(4+9\\text{i})(4-9\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={4}^{2}+(4)(-9)\\text{i}+(4)(9)\\text{i}-{\\text{i}}^{2}(9)^{2} \\\\  & =16-36\\text{i}+36\\text{i}-(-1)(81) \\\\  & =16+81 \\\\  & =97.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284473 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059808 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "27da4eb7-48c8-58f4-9676-c88d659f5fbb" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing an Imaginary Number by a Complex Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01599038-7fa1-5afe-a124-32486980db61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{26}{41}-\\frac{45}{41}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281699} {:answer/id #uuid "094a0436-bb98-5af8-8458-8b8973d56489" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{36}{41}-\\frac{45}{41}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281771} {:answer/id #uuid "1edfa3f9-dec2-598c-8e3c-7d00d0bf3742" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{36}{41}+\\frac{45}{41}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281965} {:answer/id #uuid "323d7b35-d6d0-5feb-94bc-bfcb12c5c230" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{36}{41}-\\frac{45}{41}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282141} {:answer/id #uuid "60789803-7419-5718-9d75-986c99c7bf03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{36}{41}+\\frac{45}{41}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282506}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "094a0436-bb98-5af8-8458-8b8973d56489" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{36}{41}-\\frac{45}{41}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281771} :answer-field/id #uuid "4808f53c-9dff-528d-a08d-990f1659c668" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284051}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2e45dca1-bb14-5a43-81c1-494f270d1180" :question/math-academy-id "q-16433" :question/problem "$\\frac{9\\text{i}}{-5+4\\text{i}}=$" :question/worked-solution "The denominator is $-5+4\\text{i}$ and its conjugate is $-5-4\\text{i}.$ We multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $-5-4\\text{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9\\text{i}}{-5+4\\text{i}} & =\\frac{9\\text{i}}{-5+4\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{-5-4\\text{i}}{-5-4\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-45\\text{i}-36{\\text{i}}^{2}}{{5}^{2}-{4}^{2}{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-45\\text{i}-36(-1)}{{5}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{36-45\\text{i}}{25+16} \\\\  & =\\frac{36-45\\text{i}}{41}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{36-45\\text{i}}{41}=\\frac{36}{41}-\\frac{45}{41}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284475 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ace3c4b0-6b1c-595d-bc60-b89e2b24f6b4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Asymptotes of a Vertical Hyperbola Given in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27cf303d-bbd6-5a0a-b641-f24761a186cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+1=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282031} {:answer/id #uuid "3c9af66e-e8ab-5330-970d-d985ef0bfcf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-1=\\pm \\sqrt{3}(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282221} {:answer/id #uuid "8147e6af-79cc-5b38-a020-202878bc29bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+1=\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282801} {:answer/id #uuid "dd297c6f-7df7-5f86-b8b6-ab58bbcbca30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-1=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283617} {:answer/id #uuid "e3b9a4ed-b340-577f-8ff3-b6abde0a73e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+1=\\pm \\sqrt{2}(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283676}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27cf303d-bbd6-5a0a-b641-f24761a186cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+1=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282031} :answer-field/id #uuid "865a28e7-3257-5dae-a154-35f7414ee83c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284166}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "04eeeae4-c27a-580c-95e7-3112d95f585b" :question/math-academy-id "q-16436" :question/problem "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{3}-\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{2}=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The standard equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nFor a vertical hyperbola, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\na=\\sqrt{3},\\,b=\\sqrt{2},\\,h=-1,\\,k=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-1) & =\\pm (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{2}})(x-(-1)) \\\\ y+1 & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}(x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401537 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087841 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e43e393-8752-58bc-b87c-b72f679cbfd9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Lengths of the Major and Minor Axes Given the Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "755a881c-6b14-5e96-bd68-6af58d5ef813" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401539} {:answer/id #uuid "5965e390-56a0-55ef-a7dc-eff34c09cc53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{2}$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401540} {:answer/id #uuid "1f629da2-c549-5180-8903-3a78b8767616" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4\\sqrt{2}$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401541} {:answer/id #uuid "1c1fc5c2-bdfa-52e9-a59e-c6227d171448" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401542} {:answer/id #uuid "47a16fa9-9d49-55d0-a143-c2b6cca5c4dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{2}$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401543}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f629da2-c549-5180-8903-3a78b8767616" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4\\sqrt{2}$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401541} :answer-field/id #uuid "b41dd28d-0ca0-5222-9cfe-700ddaa37f17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401538}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e791f5b6-5e18-534a-99e5-45cc16c1809f" :question/math-academy-id "q-16455" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=1$ and ${b}^{2}=8.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=1,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{1}=1.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=8,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{8}=2\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=2\\sqrt{2},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=1.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}=4\\sqrt{2},$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 1=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401558 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087834 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5028940-be34-56b7-8947-6129252f06a7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Center and Lengths of the Major and Minor Axes Given the Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8434a84-5ef1-505b-a2c7-d42809c26959" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{5}$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401560} {:answer/id #uuid "386a0708-44f8-5648-9834-907d2d7fef7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{5}$ and $\\sqrt{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401561} {:answer/id #uuid "f019f459-9c8d-5bca-ac2e-8bf08a5f03f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ and $\\sqrt{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401562} {:answer/id #uuid "1362416b-6b2e-5b41-8cce-b430bb96c908" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4\\sqrt{5}$ and $2\\sqrt{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401563} {:answer/id #uuid "c489543f-9ba5-563a-8700-e139b5d4d02f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4\\sqrt{5}$ and $\\sqrt{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401564}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1362416b-6b2e-5b41-8cce-b430bb96c908" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4\\sqrt{5}$ and $2\\sqrt{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401563} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9fd9c31-b922-5bc4-b3d4-983574117b6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401559}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8cb21ae0-f2f0-5250-97c3-5094275c61e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-16465" :question/problem "For the ellipse \n$\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{20}=1$, find the lengths of its major and minor axes." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=-1$ and $k=-3,$ so the center is located at $(-1,-3).$\n\nIn the given equation, we also have ${a}^{2}=5$ and ${b}^{2}=20.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=5,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{5}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=20,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{20}=2\\sqrt{5}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=2\\sqrt{5},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=\\sqrt{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{5}=4\\sqrt{5},$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\sqrt{5}=2\\sqrt{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284523 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198436 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b19cc766-bdc5-5ab2-aa4d-ebddf8311116" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating a Multivariable Function at a Point Using a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f8a1f57-6edf-5d51-9b7c-2d9bbd429811" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281829} {:answer/id #uuid "13106fba-e2d8-590f-9a50-2b1d63582d06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{4}{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281872} {:answer/id #uuid "1abdb628-18f3-5897-af88-0cc7b944c171" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281934} {:answer/id #uuid "2a19cffe-821c-5512-84e1-9848e5f50a0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282054} {:answer/id #uuid "fcf50d5a-a34b-5ce3-bd0f-8c170f17a78a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283907}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f8a1f57-6edf-5d51-9b7c-2d9bbd429811" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281829} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d02b8fb-22de-53d2-a4aa-1c9d236c7ce2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284036}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "af702ba5-d262-51dd-8530-cde831134efb" :question/math-academy-id "q-164830" :question/problem "For the twice-differentiable function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ we have the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =0, & \\,{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}]$ to approximate the value of $f(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $a=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}],$ and the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a) & =0, & \\,\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}], & \\,\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈0+[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ y-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ y-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ y-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the polynomial at $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1) & ≈0+[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ 1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ 1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ 1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ 1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ -(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =-{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284528 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119946 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "297a4812-a87a-592a-818a-8c8d3d3f7868" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Tangent Vector is Perpendicular to Another Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e651f70-c5ab-567f-a036-5f7d5fda5c87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281963} {:answer/id #uuid "44e6520f-63d5-561c-a5db-3cf7509b2166" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,\\sqrt{5})$ and $(1,\\sqrt{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282273} {:answer/id #uuid "91d3ac21-ee56-5c77-adf0-9225f010ea01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,\\sqrt{2})$ and $(2,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282950} {:answer/id #uuid "a797455e-01a6-53b3-844b-b3b66ee0a8e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283146} {:answer/id #uuid "f7c70d08-002d-5332-bd5a-e3accf2c0b79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\sqrt{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283858}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a797455e-01a6-53b3-844b-b3b66ee0a8e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283146} :answer-field/id #uuid "821576ac-c30f-5e83-a325-353b73ea58d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284153}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "94fb91b9-85ca-5af1-8e63-64ced0f72975" :question/math-academy-id "q-164838" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=(t+2)\\,i+\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,j$, find all of the points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are perpendicular." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+2)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1})\\,j \\\\  & =i+\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ [(t+2)\\,i+\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,j]\\cdot (i+\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}\\,j) & =0 \\\\ (t+2)\\cdot 1+\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\cdot \\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}} & =0 \\\\ t+2+t & =0 \\\\ 2t+2 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $t=-1.$ \n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-1)=(-1+2)\\,i+\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+1}\\,j=i+\\sqrt{2}\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n we conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(1,\\sqrt{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398604 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119945 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4636870-89db-5bf9-8a04-777407957e33" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Tangent Vector is Parallel to Another Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26585b8c-e264-5ec5-9955-637ca3eab6f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(14,30)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398606} {:answer/id #uuid "8ffd5745-f4c8-5a19-adc0-b37920b54995" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $(\\frac{11}{2},12)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398607} {:answer/id #uuid "5225db26-fbee-5e92-ab23-1c2785ab7665" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{11}{2},12)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398608} {:answer/id #uuid "de5685b5-e86e-5a26-8d7a-36145d147fe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{5}{2},-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398609} {:answer/id #uuid "1e4aa85c-9ff1-574b-86fb-ac6e431fa3ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $(-\\frac{5}{2},-6)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398610}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de5685b5-e86e-5a26-8d7a-36145d147fe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{5}{2},-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398609} :answer-field/id #uuid "7805dc28-4db0-584d-a7f0-aae461b099c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "29b73a1c-11c1-5790-9fa9-ce94ade43d27" :question/math-academy-id "q-165039" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}+4t,3{t}^{2}+9t⟩,$ find the point at which the tangent to the curve is parallel to the vector $⟨1,3⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}+4t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}+9t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3t+4,6t+9⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,3⟩$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})=c⟨1,3⟩.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =c⟨1,3⟩ \\\\ ⟨3t+4,6t+9⟩ & =c⟨1,3⟩ \\\\ ⟨3t+4,6t+9⟩ & =⟨c,3c⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3t+4=c \\\\ 6t+9=3c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain that $c=3t+4.$ Substituting $c=3t+4$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6t+9 & =3(3t+4) \\\\ 6t+9 & =9t+12 \\\\ 3t & =-3 \\\\ t & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $⟨1,3⟩$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=-1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-1) & =⟨\\frac{3}{2}(-1)^{2}+4(-1),3(-1)^{2}+9(-1)⟩=⟨-\\frac{5}{2},-6⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨1,3⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $(-\\frac{5}{2},-6).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284533 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119945 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4636870-89db-5bf9-8a04-777407957e33" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Tangent Vector is Parallel to Another Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62673200-4c0a-5263-85f9-353118030270" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,1,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282532} {:answer/id #uuid "6fe62f5a-876d-5428-a4fd-1f95c3435a0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,1,-10)$ and $(-1,1,6)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282647} {:answer/id #uuid "a2a8f53f-5d5d-53ec-8852-15d2b0af2998" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,1,-10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283109} {:answer/id #uuid "d9878cca-d5ca-5837-8e22-aeb433638308" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ and $(0,4,8)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283585} {:answer/id #uuid "f42fb8e1-f60f-5f7d-befe-18582fd60dbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,4,8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283822}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f42fb8e1-f60f-5f7d-befe-18582fd60dbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,4,8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283822} :answer-field/id #uuid "94bcf6b7-5c8a-5a4a-8738-115f0e77bda9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284190}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "41750cbe-5e6f-57d4-bbba-88d4814fe1e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-165040" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2}-2t\\,,\\,{t}^{2}\\,,\\,8t-2{t}^{2}⟩,$ find the point at which the tangent to the curve is parallel to the vector $⟨1\\,,\\,2\\,,\\,0⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8t-2{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t-2,\\,2t,\\,8-4t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,3⟩$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})=c\\,⟨1\\,,\\,2\\,,\\,0⟩.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =c\\,⟨1\\,,\\,2\\,,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2t-2,\\,2t,\\,8-4t⟩ & =c\\,⟨1\\,,\\,2\\,,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2t-2,\\,2t,\\,8-4t⟩ & =⟨c\\,,\\,2c\\,,\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2t-2=c \\\\ 2t=2c \\\\ 8-4t=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we obtain that $t=2.$ Substituting to the second equation, we get $c=2.$\nThese values also satisfy the first equation. \n\nTherefore, $⟨1\\,,\\,2\\,,\\,0⟩$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=2.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(2) & =⟨{2}^{2}-2(2)\\,,\\,{2}^{2}\\,,\\,8(2)-2(2)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,4,\\,8⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨1\\,,\\,2\\,,\\,0⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $(0,4,8).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284535 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119945 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4636870-89db-5bf9-8a04-777407957e33" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Tangent Vector is Parallel to Another Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05b526ee-e645-545d-9d38-eba3bf488f13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(e,\\frac{5}{4})$ and $({e}^{-2},\\frac{1}{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281738} {:answer/id #uuid "14e0f6aa-69fa-5a2a-a5a2-05425a8ac3c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281883} {:answer/id #uuid "5c2c1ef9-8c20-5ce3-95b9-c866ecb62eb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({e}^{2},\\frac{5}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282469} {:answer/id #uuid "944b8abb-39ab-55bb-808e-d4b88ed9adfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,\\frac{1}{2})$ and $({e}^{2},\\frac{5}{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282963} {:answer/id #uuid "bb28ac5d-2a0c-5e78-8e08-1b94f7e7ed42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(e,\\frac{5}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283305}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14e0f6aa-69fa-5a2a-a5a2-05425a8ac3c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281883} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0b7424d-a230-5a57-a288-0f3d6f9aa64c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284358}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c1d5d096-073b-5c49-80fa-a124c3af6414" :question/math-academy-id "q-165042" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)={e}^{2t}\\,i+({t}^{2}+t+\\frac{1}{2})\\,j$, find all points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+t+\\frac{1}{2})\\,j \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}\\,i+(2t+1)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that $r({t}_{0})=c\\,{r}^{′}({t}_{0}).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =c\\,{r}^{′}(t) \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\,i+({t}^{2}+t+\\frac{1}{2})\\,j & =c(2{e}^{2t}\\,i+(2t+1)\\,j) \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\,i+({t}^{2}+t+\\frac{1}{2})\\,j & =2c{e}^{2t}\\,i+c(2t+1)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $i$ and $j$ gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t}=2c{e}^{2t} \\\\ {t}^{2}+t+\\frac{1}{2}=c(2t+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain $c=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Substituting $c=\\frac{1}{2}$ into the second equality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}+t+\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(2t+1) \\\\ {t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ t & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $r$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel at the point corresponding to $t=0.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & ={e}^{0}\\,i+({0}^{2}+0+\\frac{1}{2})\\,j \\\\  & =i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $(1,\\frac{1}{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284548 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096708 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e182f71-db24-5c29-9c8b-2fc4b5ab5dd8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Y-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c70e801-fd99-586e-ad50-fca199a9d991" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281802} {:answer/id #uuid "99578261-0e54-5ddf-9cbe-2131732d1331" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283022} {:answer/id #uuid "b33f1a06-1ff7-5054-9b68-11c4a5d9ab9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283244} {:answer/id #uuid "cacc8365-ba97-5758-a9a1-2e6465cbb4d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283463} {:answer/id #uuid "fca7c681-e646-5b9c-9404-16ca07e77a34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283903}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b33f1a06-1ff7-5054-9b68-11c4a5d9ab9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283244} :answer-field/id #uuid "e67bd542-ad74-5a89-8312-e4d33f763205" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a86ebeb5-18d1-5f84-a46f-2e3c446ad259" :question/math-academy-id "q-165433" :question/problem "![](images/61/616116b9e5cf137c526b11dc5ce1647cd8f9cbd3a652be6dade9a8769467c27c.png)\n\nWhat is the $y$-intercept of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(5,15)$ and passes through the point $(2,6).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(5,15)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(x-5)^{2}=4p(y-15).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(2,6),$ we substitute $x=2$ and $y=6$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2-5)^{2} & =4p(6-15) \\\\ 9 & =4p(-9) \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-5)^{2} & =-(y-15).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-5)^{2} & =-(y-15) \\\\ 25 & =-y+15 \\\\ y & =15-25 \\\\ y & =-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $-10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284552 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092281 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d19a514c-faa5-5f69-9870-87f78878f08a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Asymptotes of a Vertical Hyperbola by Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fba9bd5-4745-5c15-9e73-09dda07c45e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+2=\\pm \\frac{1}{2}(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281832} {:answer/id #uuid "27d1e720-8815-5954-a527-c6200c0d42b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+2=\\pm 2(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282032} {:answer/id #uuid "85c8ba05-1e27-53e7-9424-59fb54d63b21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+1=\\pm \\frac{1}{4}(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282844} {:answer/id #uuid "9c85b62f-f927-594c-af51-ccb941e86b48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-1=\\pm 4(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283052} {:answer/id #uuid "d217d63c-5015-5dfe-b9be-334ec4f0fb14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-1=\\pm \\frac{1}{2}(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283519}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d217d63c-5015-5dfe-b9be-334ec4f0fb14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-1=\\pm \\frac{1}{2}(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283519} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a6ba17a-4353-5e49-a520-101c5f637f40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284057}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5dc3ef25-ecd7-5c57-8a7f-d09fad78e669" :question/math-academy-id "q-165482" :question/problem "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $4{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}-8y-4x-16=0.$" :question/worked-solution "To rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in standard form, we need to group the $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}-8y-4x-16 & =0 \\\\ 4{y}^{2}-8y-{x}^{2}-4x-16 & =0 \\\\ 4[{y}^{2}-2y]-[{x}^{2}+4x]-16 & =0 \\\\ 4[(y-1)^{2}-1]-[(x+2)^{2}-4]-16 & =0 \\\\ 4(y-1)^{2}-4-(x+2)^{2}+4-16 & =0 \\\\ 4(y-1)^{2}-(x+2)^{2} & =16 \\\\ \\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{16}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe asymptotes of a vertical hyperbola\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by \n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n(h,k)=(-2,1),\\,a=\\sqrt{4}=2,\\,b=\\sqrt{16}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\ny-1=\\pm \\frac{1}{2}(x+2).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284557 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087727 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d58fba3c-4bbf-5a74-a911-18db101eb632" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Center and Axis Lengths Given a Homogeneous Polynomial Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a0df234-c3b6-5275-bb55-70afecc12dcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282318} {:answer/id #uuid "73e8f492-0bf9-55fe-8d92-a94b65a8a1bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282679} {:answer/id #uuid "852acf03-ef6d-5d5a-93f1-5a863b5cd35f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282838} {:answer/id #uuid "ae906ad3-e496-5dba-89a5-dc17369dcfbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283198} {:answer/id #uuid "ccd6b550-eb59-5d55-bc61-e0518c41c707" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283479}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a0df234-c3b6-5275-bb55-70afecc12dcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282318} :answer-field/id #uuid "62372821-ae81-53cd-908a-823dfcb9b5ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284096}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5ed2b043-750a-5637-b0ae-a564424a079c" :question/math-academy-id "q-16561" :question/problem "Determine the length of the minor axis of the ellipse whose equation is  $25{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+100x–40y+100=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+100x–40y+100 & =0 \\\\ (25{x}^{2}+100x)+(4{y}^{2}-40y)+100 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(25{x}^{2}+100x)+(4{y}^{2}-40y)+100 & =0 \\\\ 25[{x}^{2}+4x]+4[{y}^{2}-10y]+100 & =0 \\\\ 25[(x+2)^{2}-{2}^{2}]+4[(y-5)^{2}-{5}^{2}]+100 & =0 \\\\ 25[(x+2)^{2}-4]+4[(y-5)^{2}-25]+100 & =0 \\\\ 25(x+2)^{2}-100+4(y-5)^{2}-100+100 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25(x+2)^{2}-100+4(y-5)^{2}-100+100 & =0 \\\\ 25(x+2)^{2}+4(y-5)^{2} & =-100+100+100 \\\\ 25(x+2)^{2}+4(y-5)^{2} & =100 \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $5>2,$ the length of the minor axis is $2\\cdot 2=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284559 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087727 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d58fba3c-4bbf-5a74-a911-18db101eb632" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Center and Axis Lengths Given a Homogeneous Polynomial Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22961d8f-86d9-5ab2-b42e-0012e783a23f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281993} {:answer/id #uuid "2b13c0a9-a51b-523a-9ce4-3254dce6faef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282067} {:answer/id #uuid "8666c22c-5eb3-5056-869a-853adc498f85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282848} {:answer/id #uuid "959b5fee-bb15-5499-82bb-5faf30029096" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282980} {:answer/id #uuid "9c88853b-9cd6-530a-ad8a-4bf8ba0d8592" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283053}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b13c0a9-a51b-523a-9ce4-3254dce6faef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282067} :answer-field/id #uuid "2871ec54-332b-5cf4-8084-1f8c2e08bc69" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284007}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "beb4f1e9-9ce4-5e8f-a547-7adfbf588a59" :question/math-academy-id "q-16565" :question/problem "Determine the length of the major axis of the ellipse whose equation is ${x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+10x-16y+25=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+10x-16y+25 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+10x)+(4{y}^{2}-16y)+25 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}+10x)+(4{y}^{2}-16y)+25 & =0 \\\\ [{x}^{2}+10x]+4[{y}^{2}-4y]+25 & =0 \\\\ [(x+5)^{2}-{5}^{2}]+4[(y-2)^{2}-{2}^{2}]+25 & =0 \\\\ [(x+5)^{2}-25]+4[(y-2)^{2}-4]+25 & =0 \\\\ (x+5)^{2}-25+4(y-2)^{2}-16+25 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+5)^{2}-25+4(y-2)^{2}-16+25 & =0 \\\\ (x+5)^{2}+4(y-2)^{2} & =25-25+16 \\\\ (x+5)^{2}+4(y-2)^{2} & =16 \\\\ \\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $4>2,$ the length of the major axis is $2\\cdot 4=8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284562 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115205 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7df2ec3-6138-5b84-864b-a856c28b9747" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Y-Intercept of a Transformed Exponential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2432ea9b-d21e-5a51-8b4f-b55efb6bd9e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282007} {:answer/id #uuid "c0cbd424-f3a1-5a84-9361-9c96b4e6b238" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283363} {:answer/id #uuid "c2a95d55-7f7d-5b96-9047-fa934daf6737" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283384} {:answer/id #uuid "d086b4fb-f27d-5ee6-8b86-c8312d6c931f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283508} {:answer/id #uuid "e1eef64f-270a-5804-b5e7-c47f798b2c54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283659}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2432ea9b-d21e-5a51-8b4f-b55efb6bd9e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282007} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2a3e1d9-5970-53e9-bf11-467d12c1c3c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284278}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f7bdbdc9-a156-56fb-bd3a-e5d66cea5150" :question/math-academy-id "q-165971" :question/problem "Determine the $y$-intercept of $y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5x-2}+1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5(0)-2}+1 \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{-2}+1 \\\\  & ={2}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =4+1 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389567 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105767 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69bac3fa-d409-5c16-87b2-383423e8d754" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Square Under a Shear or Stretch Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a71980a-a780-533c-8292-ac7c714bcb68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/36/364633d020e330780fab5ea588cca231ecd0e3d3c8e3e5516e4d6d0d990299c0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389569} {:answer/id #uuid "30676351-f4d0-5879-a162-25816a196693" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/da/dae63d7007d115438139be51862d878deadd1ed300b3b870935c2cbe44edaef3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389570} {:answer/id #uuid "bf91d35b-3b4c-5341-8e3c-574c51f569f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/46/46e48817af724645b2dde55f6aa55a48c44b5160de6aebec0b1da5cc7ef1e79c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389571} {:answer/id #uuid "5c0ada63-f751-502d-996f-08e36d9763eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bf/bfdc9898972e72a5d15c8313eff3403e2ea712e2bffe52b3fd1d9fb6156b7bec.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389572} {:answer/id #uuid "bb18aec9-e801-5b41-b04e-dfaa22bace09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ad/adaef18865941a58a89a221c40f8e44aa6b95def80d12a08897c7be34ae7a9aa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389573}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c0ada63-f751-502d-996f-08e36d9763eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bf/bfdc9898972e72a5d15c8313eff3403e2ea712e2bffe52b3fd1d9fb6156b7bec.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389572} :answer-field/id #uuid "019c3753-a69c-52d8-a6b6-c6496d15982e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389568}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "80a9d60b-2d00-5629-b0f1-438f9ef2a843" :question/math-academy-id "q-165980" :question/problem "![](images/61/611f958a7bd7475e37f743ef86affde7754195fe2c9ad414e628c17a09d47e38.png)\n\nLet $T=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ be the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ What is the image of the square $R$ (shown above) under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Looking at the matrix $T$, we can see that $T$ represents a horizontal shear of shear factor $1.$ Therefore, the image of the square $R$ under $T$ is\n\n  \n\n![](images/cf/cfe398e1ab7e5b4b487671772bf4b6127e8e141b25491f96c5cc32e77869dec0.png)\n\n**Alternative method:**\n\nWe can check that the vertices of $R$ are mapped to the vertices of the parallelogram shown above as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401565 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087725 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a508c1-2379-590f-85f5-386a1990f137" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the x-Intercepts of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4f3d642-de3f-54d5-aede-eba21e11be92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is no $x$-intercept" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401567} {:answer/id #uuid "e9df0568-122c-5b6d-9b84-d5caa3cabe43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401568} {:answer/id #uuid "dc20f4f3-9c00-5d0b-920a-7a632bc6b622" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401569} {:answer/id #uuid "20209ee4-eb61-55e6-aa7a-3caa61c198e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401570} {:answer/id #uuid "73f81d60-d0cf-5e03-8299-56a8163c5850" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401571}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc20f4f3-9c00-5d0b-920a-7a632bc6b622" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401569} :answer-field/id #uuid "160e9ede-f9e3-593e-8295-6afb981450c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401566}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fca3c3f7-188d-5506-9818-be66c0607803" :question/math-academy-id "q-16642" :question/problem "Calculate the points where the ellipse $\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9}=1$ intercepts the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(0-2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{4}{9} & =1 \\\\ 9(x-3)^{2}+16 & =36 \\\\ 9(x-3)^{2} & =20 \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =\\frac{20}{9} \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{20}{9}} \\\\ x & =3\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at the points $(3-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3},0)$ and $(3+\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3},0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401572 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087725 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a508c1-2379-590f-85f5-386a1990f137" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the x-Intercepts of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e455adf-7973-5ee4-bb3c-0bd83316580a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is no $x$-intercept" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401574} {:answer/id #uuid "d3568acd-90d7-5e24-95a2-7dddb6de4815" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1\\pm \\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401575} {:answer/id #uuid "d33bd951-6cf6-508d-8421-15a11016a317" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1\\pm \\frac{10\\sqrt{2}}{3},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401576} {:answer/id #uuid "829db562-f4d5-5e31-b45c-b397742cb7e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\pm \\sqrt{5},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401577} {:answer/id #uuid "a69ed72a-53dd-5923-8c05-9efa0f54638b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1\\pm \\frac{10\\sqrt{2}}{3},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401578}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d33bd951-6cf6-508d-8421-15a11016a317" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1\\pm \\frac{10\\sqrt{2}}{3},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401576} :answer-field/id #uuid "d58b31e7-b6c4-5d90-9a58-b8914d59f384" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401573}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ba3205c4-c133-5ac7-861e-655b59a14526" :question/math-academy-id "q-16645" :question/problem "Calculate the points where the ellipse $\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{9}=1$ intercepts the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(0-1)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{1}{9} & =1 \\\\ 9(x+1)^{2}+25 & =9\\cdot 25 \\\\ 9(x+1)^{2}+25 & =225 \\\\ 9(x+1)^{2} & =200 \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =\\frac{200}{9} \\\\ x+1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{200}{9}} \\\\ x & =-1\\pm \\frac{10\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at the points $(-1-\\frac{10\\sqrt{2}}{3},0)$ and $(-1+\\frac{10\\sqrt{2}}{3},0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284568 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087725 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a508c1-2379-590f-85f5-386a1990f137" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the x-Intercepts of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68d1becd-834d-5b48-8a24-ab24c44b2a5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\pm 3,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282587} {:answer/id #uuid "afa421db-4a22-51a3-be0c-e9fc90b73e74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3\\pm 3\\sqrt{2},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283208} {:answer/id #uuid "c457d605-1675-5574-80ea-b02b08321902" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\pm \\sqrt{3},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283402} {:answer/id #uuid "dd86044f-0ff0-52c7-a162-ab49488967da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is no $x$-intercept" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283623} {:answer/id #uuid "f1f0a86d-eee6-5481-8c8b-c4472e4f786e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283799}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "afa421db-4a22-51a3-be0c-e9fc90b73e74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3\\pm 3\\sqrt{2},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283208} :answer-field/id #uuid "037f6be5-b84d-5111-bcea-8a60d5bd8ffd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283938}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ad41fa9c-427e-53d4-84ac-70d9cefddf98" :question/math-academy-id "q-16647" :question/problem "Calculate the points where the ellipse $4(x-3)^{2}+9{y}^{2}=72$ intercepts the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-3)^{2}+9{y}^{2} & =72 \\\\ 4(x-3)^{2}+9\\cdot {0}^{2} & =72 \\\\ 4(x-3)^{2} & =72 \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =18 \\\\ (x-3) & =\\pm \\sqrt{18} \\\\ (x-3) & =\\pm 3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ x & =3\\pm 3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at $(3-3\\sqrt{2},0)$ and $(3+3\\sqrt{2},0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284570 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115207 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c21c1eca-cf80-56bd-9f44-aab7989cb210" :knowledge-point/title "The Root of a Transformed Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e3c4350-ac40-5ff9-8a09-56250f13df9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282483} {:answer/id #uuid "70df90bc-b41f-53c9-ace7-14daa143036c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282655} {:answer/id #uuid "7268a672-d5da-52a3-9409-8ef22f8f8007" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282668} {:answer/id #uuid "aca0a2ac-645b-50e7-b37a-d45fee6649df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283184} {:answer/id #uuid "da63b78c-2331-50f1-a0b0-42446f4d812e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283593}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aca0a2ac-645b-50e7-b37a-d45fee6649df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283184} :answer-field/id #uuid "85e23c55-3564-5b82-9ccb-0a33e23e13b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284162}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b15a7e63-b8ab-5083-9941-85a35f55da3d" :question/math-academy-id "q-166549" :question/problem "Find the root of $f(x)={log}_{2}⁡(4-2x)+1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the root of $f(x)$, we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n{log}_{2}⁡(4-2x)+1=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡(4-2x)+1 & =0 \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(4-2x) & =-1 \\\\ {2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(4-2x)} & ={2}^{-1} \\\\ 4-2x & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -2x & =-\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{7}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=\\frac{7}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389574 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217152 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5fa86dd3-3bb3-54d5-8940-5dc07ae4c99f" :knowledge-point/title "Exponential Growth Functions With Horizontal and Vertical Shifts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99ad0535-275d-5569-9ac3-9c14fa99a5fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/11/11ec7fbc5908465c69f2bea959f6164ab115b30f767260652658649bc7e9cf65.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389576} {:answer/id #uuid "c97a6005-11ae-5bc5-9656-338b6e77d9cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/83/83ee71cbcf2456ce581e91624155d9528903c56c491debdbc3de392e7799720a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389577} {:answer/id #uuid "5731da45-7ae4-5755-be3e-c71696c160cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/10/1065343505af421e2a1fc06c9733dfc3a2354842daec12ac4bcc2d7935c8b1ab.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389578} {:answer/id #uuid "6eb0e59b-b1ef-56d1-b881-1e0d2f7f1ccd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c2/c2d437d87e38ef73849987c358bc00fd589535c5045b193cdfa54ecc9601f37e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389579} {:answer/id #uuid "18a565a2-df94-546c-9080-271b1c1bf113" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8c/8ccfcbd3042f4504a2df8f51c82df1238fdc6c479fa99f6b4691543f8685ffba.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389580}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6eb0e59b-b1ef-56d1-b881-1e0d2f7f1ccd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c2/c2d437d87e38ef73849987c358bc00fd589535c5045b193cdfa54ecc9601f37e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389579} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fbff92d-a67a-5b5a-a0bc-1673af73baa4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389575}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e46ce850-a38a-5466-abbe-3315ac65e081" :question/math-academy-id "q-167626" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y={7}^{x+2}-3?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- First, we plot the curve $y={7}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/4b/4b2274f6e19ea02b6ebee1d9ac590c8186722be946f1d390ae4d27b2898d883e.png)\n\n- To plot $y={7}^{x+2},$ we shift the graph of $y={7}^{x}$ horizontally by $2$ units to the left. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(-2,1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/d9/d900437ffd3ba0da1c6c25cbcff892e76929abe839fc8a49abea555c8163714f.png)\n\n- To plot the graph $y={7}^{x+2}-3,$ we shift the graph of $y={7}^{x+2}$ vertically by $3$ units down. The point $(-2,1)$ moves to $(-2,-2)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-3.$\n\n![](images/3c/3c8f16780a5d9e3b655b9c7706fc664e9ef944d728d070c9cca886ac05b34504.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389581 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115369 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "623f4440-ba1a-58ce-9ff2-ccdf591a0d30" :knowledge-point/title "Vertical Reflections of Exponential Decay Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e91d68c4-e924-5c0b-8892-337b390a9278" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ca/cadd20667d93ddc9058703331d2442573b4febc0eb79b0c50749490a859d7006.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389583} {:answer/id #uuid "d252b9b0-032b-5d42-9ac1-9f37846b1397" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/44/4442c3b03668bf3d4b8090c56c4ad083e85aa4399a0326255fe2322f2dcc729b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389584} {:answer/id #uuid "d19a1858-cafb-517d-ba52-283fda091cec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/77/779c8384569a3cf6cec79186e69a657b4e23f98f2dd464a3879700b4c08fd27a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389585} {:answer/id #uuid "b9429aad-0aa1-50e2-95f4-b8ee60295084" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b9/b934a5fcadb46619e6c3631e6f9d78c8a0f72beeb023f9696c01422503188586.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389586} {:answer/id #uuid "9f9ba8eb-d544-5202-91c3-233917e73327" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ee/eed323df68b293af1fa8ec575d4dca23f16fe01865b01c3fa9817eead7b66fd2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389587}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d19a1858-cafb-517d-ba52-283fda091cec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/77/779c8384569a3cf6cec79186e69a657b4e23f98f2dd464a3879700b4c08fd27a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389585} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba892752-d68e-5265-b5cc-644accea6428" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389582}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1f2385ee-c24f-5a4c-9712-f5717ae06cfb" :question/math-academy-id "q-167668" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}-3?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/e9/e992d80b1245e6fad1c1e2968c396c51ef863d56d36c184592f8025fc450fa99.png)\n\n- Reflect it in the $x$-axis to give $y=-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,-1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/aa/aa0778c338e9b57aca82057edc1b984e051d05a3f5413c2b1c70c3874fcf063e.png)\n\n- Then, vertically shift it by $3$ units down to give $y=-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}-3.$ The point $(0,-1)$ moves to $(0,-4)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from from $y=0$ to $y=-3.$\n\n![](images/25/25b4ef6f489f19d81b1d54a3240faf5e99f20a9463aa2ce9bd2e39796bf39cb3.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284599 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059807 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "730fc9a7-a1b8-55a4-b8d3-666f87b82958" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing a Complex Number by an Imaginary Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0107644a-636e-50e4-b930-e17a354fdcca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}-\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281697} {:answer/id #uuid "1f4ff1eb-f9a7-5e3c-8ac0-e54f3c09fa36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}+\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281968} {:answer/id #uuid "5216a17c-ee4d-569d-a009-6f2c9c02ea61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282397} {:answer/id #uuid "a1ee21cf-2523-53e6-9fc6-1f13574ba3e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\frac{2}{5}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283101} {:answer/id #uuid "dfe5810c-fd56-56b1-a811-91d5fac7cb1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{5}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283645}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0107644a-636e-50e4-b930-e17a354fdcca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}-\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281697} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f63666b-a8b4-518b-baa1-f96fb9218571" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284147}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3823f12d-a05b-542e-bce7-ac4c9e2e0055" :question/math-academy-id "q-167677" :question/problem "$\\frac{5+2\\text{i}}{5\\text{i}}=$" :question/worked-solution "Here, the denominator is the imaginary number $5\\text{i}.$ Note that this can be written as $0+5\\text{i},$ so the conjugate is $0-5\\text{i},$ or simply $-5\\text{i}.$\n\nSo, we multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $-5\\text{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5+2\\text{i}}{5\\text{i}} & =\\frac{5+2\\text{i}}{5\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{-5\\text{i}}{-5\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-25\\text{i}-10{\\text{i}}^{2}}{-25{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-25\\text{i}-10(-1)}{-25(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{10-25\\text{i}}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{10-25\\text{i}}{25} & =\\frac{10}{25}-\\frac{25}{25}\\text{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284613 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115286 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0236acfd-59d1-5bc8-9e60-1c76ddc536f5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Equation Given a Complex Root and Its Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22224482-71e5-503e-aeb0-1389c520e9fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}-13x+4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281990} {:answer/id #uuid "31836b42-2e59-5fce-8442-89d4fd7c9f1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}+4x+13=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282134} {:answer/id #uuid "4c698298-8851-5d55-89ba-e22931b280af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}+16x+13=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282343} {:answer/id #uuid "7b91bc17-590d-568e-ac2d-3d145f902ae6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}-8x+16=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282752} {:answer/id #uuid "bcf9f3c9-1308-5b1f-b923-3d6e3bf1521d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}-4x+13=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283328}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bcf9f3c9-1308-5b1f-b923-3d6e3bf1521d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}-4x+13=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283328} :answer-field/id #uuid "7923737a-702a-5f5a-9411-30659b3e5ae9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284141}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f148df04-75e0-5664-8af5-e27f09e24675" :question/math-academy-id "q-16935" :question/problem "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=\\frac{1}{2}+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $4$, find the equation." :question/worked-solution "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=\\frac{1}{2}+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=\\frac{1}{2}-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-(\\frac{1}{2}+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}))(x-(\\frac{1}{2}-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}))=0\n$$\n\nBy expanding the parentheses, we can write the equation in standard form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x(\\frac{1}{2}-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i})-x(\\frac{1}{2}+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i})+(\\frac{1}{2}+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i})(\\frac{1}{2}-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{x}{2}+x\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}-\\frac{x}{2}-x\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}+\\frac{1}{4}-3{\\text{i}}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{4}+3 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-x+\\frac{13}{4} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\text{i},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $4.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $4.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({x}^{2}-x+\\frac{13}{4}) & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-4x+13 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284620 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119940 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0305491f-5ac7-5ba4-9a80-1a9a79b964f0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating dy/dx at a Point Using dx/dy"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e89892f-6177-5da8-ba8f-f6ad4938f860" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281816} {:answer/id #uuid "12b70b84-7322-57f9-a48d-b97394706f4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281867} {:answer/id #uuid "8ced86e9-fb84-576d-be70-7a5432a09c98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282911} {:answer/id #uuid "cd423368-a8a3-578c-8fba-929065592761" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283482} {:answer/id #uuid "f43a7ca7-597b-5512-aa7c-ee8e4811dc35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283824}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd423368-a8a3-578c-8fba-929065592761" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283482} :answer-field/id #uuid "012d375b-46c2-5142-9bad-9eca1ad9eaec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283934}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0eb61aef-5332-5039-a2f0-c31d2273ab77" :question/math-academy-id "q-170140" :question/problem "If $x=y-2{y}^{3},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at the point $(1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y-2{y}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y)-2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{3}) \\\\  & =1-6{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1-6{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the point $(1,-1),$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(1,-1)} & =\\frac{1}{1-6(-1)^{2}}=-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284622 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119941 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ff302ba-5a3f-599b-b038-7417af1dca71" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Tangent Line Using dx/dy"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43180867-e54c-5b2d-baa1-cf9a1cbe0b74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{x}{8}+\\frac{33}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282261} {:answer/id #uuid "6a9f3558-657f-579f-8054-76a165cec2f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{x}{10}+\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282607} {:answer/id #uuid "772d57cb-a077-5382-a7cf-063edbf390c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{x}{8}-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282709} {:answer/id #uuid "b61872c4-87ff-5f2f-a588-6b1bb2670cc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{x}{8}+\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283270} {:answer/id #uuid "bf08dc7d-e699-5a3a-8693-a33d5378ca66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-10x+44" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283351}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b61872c4-87ff-5f2f-a588-6b1bb2670cc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{x}{8}+\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283270} :answer-field/id #uuid "5645007e-953e-5e1b-8348-3a7a0c4acd9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284078}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5409375b-690a-557c-9e51-1e26c492e709" :question/math-academy-id "q-170189" :question/problem "Given a curve $x=y+\\frac{3}{{y}^{3}},$ what is the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point $(4,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y+\\frac{3}{{y}^{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y)+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y)+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{-3}) \\\\  & =1+3\\cdot (-3{y}^{-4}) \\\\  & =1-9{y}^{-4} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{9}{{y}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{4}-9}{{y}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{-1}=\\frac{{y}^{4}}{{y}^{4}-9}.\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $(4,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(4,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{1}^{4}}{{1}^{4}-9} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point-slope formula for a tangent line, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{1}{8}(x-4) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{x}{8}+\\frac{1}{2}+1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{x}{8}+\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401593 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068900 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "66ade855-4b16-514b-b44a-1d6b9d61ef28" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift Applied to an Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a316d2cb-bab1-518a-9a6c-3956bfcbcd26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401595} {:answer/id #uuid "9ec09427-50e7-5ea6-a6c5-d462ec4a977e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}+\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401596} {:answer/id #uuid "685faf06-be0e-5f6e-869b-353ff052fead" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401597} {:answer/id #uuid "ed368aed-25dd-5df6-b266-89719aec6636" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401598} {:answer/id #uuid "d50e40c4-e8c2-5ba6-ad0e-dc0b92fad230" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "685faf06-be0e-5f6e-869b-353ff052fead" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401597} :answer-field/id #uuid "00e70783-1025-5acd-82ba-24685ceb80f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401594}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5b98dba6-8029-58a2-a81c-ae05e5ca1860" :question/math-academy-id "q-170347" :question/problem "![](images/71/71d9df0708a77639f3f7b2640ea2a03cebbfd72e6cfb3369c07d83a89eba6508.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}+c.$ Determine the value of $c$ and find the equation of the curve." :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of $y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}$ is $y=0.$ \n\nThe vertical translation $y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}+c$ shifts the asymptote to $y=c.$\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-1.$ Therefore, $c=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284628 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068831 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc402a1d-e538-5510-8a6f-79dd1626ff1f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift and Stretch Applied to an Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10110147-d53a-52b4-9412-60917bae18a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281834} {:answer/id #uuid "35ebf50d-9e51-5dd6-90ce-6b42609e1277" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282167} {:answer/id #uuid "4a227723-7044-54e3-b22e-e4d9e959d300" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282319} {:answer/id #uuid "59445ca6-d8f8-5abc-befc-bb2cef4921d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282448} {:answer/id #uuid "f114926f-bae0-59d7-a3c0-0d8adbad48cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283791}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a227723-7044-54e3-b22e-e4d9e959d300" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282319} :answer-field/id #uuid "59d527ed-62e9-59c3-b626-0a9ba794e766" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284082}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "40f36d04-c36f-53cb-8067-f12e5e811687" :question/math-academy-id "q-170350" :question/problem "![](images/06/06ea604e70390627f9e98dbea5048f27bd1f63d0b10002bbd5518c000b5125bf.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}+c.$ Find the value of $k-c.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=\\frac{4}{3},$ hence $c=\\frac{4}{3}.$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,2),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=2$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}+c & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}+\\frac{4}{3} & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{0}+\\frac{4}{3} & =2 \\\\ k+\\frac{4}{3} & =2 \\\\ k & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nk-c=\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{4}{3}=-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the curve is $y=\\frac{2}{3}{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}+\\frac{4}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284633 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064209 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "94ad8efa-33ee-5451-b6b9-c99cb81a9ba2" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Definite Integrals of Algebraic Functions Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b49dffc-ef34-51f9-880f-343a17e7f464" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{{2}^{11}}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282461} {:answer/id #uuid "8972d08b-477b-5df8-8798-490e031cacfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{{2}^{7}}}{35}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282876} {:answer/id #uuid "a2129991-a47b-5a82-ac7c-8e1ca3a89e30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{{2}^{11}}}{35}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283104} {:answer/id #uuid "b46333d5-37ec-5aa4-b7d2-c43db38be685" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{{2}^{9}}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283251} {:answer/id #uuid "c63d1f0f-ee09-585f-b726-ba8a97ddf0c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{{2}^{9}}}{35}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283415}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2129991-a47b-5a82-ac7c-8e1ca3a89e30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{{2}^{11}}}{35}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283104} :answer-field/id #uuid "54febd29-2fe4-5926-8377-9b3e1c741602" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284074}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "abf52321-10d1-5a8b-a0a2-2f3814ff9431" :question/math-academy-id "q-17045" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\sqrt{{(2-x)}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=2-x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=-\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe also note that $x=2-u.$\n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get\n\n| **$x$** | $0$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **$u$** | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nWe can now solve the integral using our new variable $u,$ remembering to use the new limits of integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\sqrt{{(2-x)}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x{(2-x)}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{0}(2-u){u}^{3/2}(-\\text{d}u) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{0}(u-2){u}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{0}({u}^{5/2}-2{u}^{3/2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={(\\frac{2}{7}{u}^{7/2}-2\\cdot \\frac{2}{5}{u}^{5/2})|}_{2}^{0} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{2}{7}{u}^{7/2}-\\frac{4}{5}{u}^{5/2})|}_{2}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{7}({0}^{7/2}-{2}^{7/2})-\\frac{4}{5}({0}^{5/2}-{2}^{5/2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{{2}^{9/2}}{7}+\\frac{{2}^{9/2}}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{11/2}}{35} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{2}^{11}}}{35}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284634 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087731 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0adde8fd-26c3-52f5-9864-5e96641a8b63" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Complex Conjugate of a Complex Number With Negative Imaginary Part"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c0f1866-e638-56a1-9509-304ec8ef6d09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{―}{z}=-7+9\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281944} {:answer/id #uuid "8102202b-2c4c-585e-b74d-fd02088bea79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{―}{z}=\\frac{1}{7-9\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282798} {:answer/id #uuid "a80ab5f1-c86c-5917-9caa-0955d8f5c929" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{―}{z}=9-7\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283150} {:answer/id #uuid "d918d600-55df-54f1-a82d-38ba06cad23b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{―}{z}=7+9\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283579} {:answer/id #uuid "fdbaec1b-1f7b-513c-9760-2c7f803ede54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{―}{z}=-7-9\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283914}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d918d600-55df-54f1-a82d-38ba06cad23b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{―}{z}=7+9\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283579} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1908e81-1d1d-58d5-be8c-ae1c94edb528" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284300}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f55201b5-4069-5034-b363-688130c1f37a" :question/math-academy-id "q-170465" :question/problem "What is the complex conjugate of $z=7-9\\text{i}?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the complex conjugate, we flip the sign of the imaginary part.\n\nIn the given number $z=7-9\\text{i},$ the imaginary part is $-9\\text{i}.$ Flipping the sign, we get $9\\text{i}.$\n\nTherefore, the complex conjugate is given by $\\overset{―}{z}=7+9\\text{i}.$"}]}
